         "browser",
         "command",
         "mcp"
       ],
       "additionalInstructions": [
         "**Schema Adherence:** Before implementing or modifying any data interaction logic, read `prisma/schema.prisma` and relevant Zod schemas (`src/schemas/*.schema.ts`). Ensure your code strictly adheres to these definitions. If a discrepancy is found between code and schema, the schema takes precedence unless a clear reason exists for the discrepancy (in which case, clarify with the Orchestrator).",
+        "**Schema Change Impact Analysis:** If a schema file (`prisma/schema.prisma`, `*.schema.ts`) is changed or detected to be changed, or if you are tasked with changing one, identify all code files that interact with the changed schema elements. Prioritize reviewing and updating these impacted files to maintain strict schema adherence.",
+        "**Code Change Impact Analysis:** Before making significant code changes, especially those affecting data models or external interfaces, identify and consider all parts of the codebase (other files, modules, tests) that might be impacted. Include these identified areas in your review and testing plan.",
         "**Test Debugging:** When tests fail: 1) Identify the failing tests and files. 2) Read the test file and the source code being tested. 3) Analyze the error messages and assertion failures to determine the root cause (code bug, mock issue, brittle assertion). 4) If mocking external dependencies (like database calls or API clients), mock the low-level calls (e.g., `prisma.model.create`, `axios.get`) rather than higher-level service functions within the tested module to ensure the internal logic is tested correctly. 5) If assertions are brittle (expecting exact large objects or precise error strings), refine them to check for key properties or use broader matches (e.g., `expect(...).toHaveProperty(...)`, `expect(...).toContain(...)`, `expect(...).toThrowError(...)`).",
+        "**Implemented Code Verification:** Before concluding work on a task, perform a self-review of the implemented code against the technical solution, acceptance criteria, and Technical Quality Rubrics. Ensure the code is clean, readable, and follows best practices, in addition to passing tests.",
         "**Code Implementation vs. Placeholders:** If you encounter tests written against placeholder code, discuss with the Orchestrator whether to implement the full logic or update the tests to accurately reflect the placeholder behavior if testing the placeholder is the task."
       ],
